ntext": "Martín del Barco Centenera spent a significant decade (1572-1582) of his life exploring and documenting the territories that would later largely comprise the Río de la Plata Viceroyalty."}, {"sourceSlug": "martn-del-barco-centenera", "sourceName": "Martín del Barco Centenera", "verb": "TRANSMITS", "targetSlug": "indigenous-cultures-of-south-america", "targetName": "Indigenous Cultures of South America", "context": "Through *La Argentina*, Martín del Barco Centenera transmitted valuable, albeit colonial-perspective, information regarding the indigenous cultures of 16th-century South America."}, {"sourceSlug": "martn-del-barco-centenera", "sourceName": "Martín del Barco Centenera", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "exploration-of-south-america", "targetName": "Exploration of South America", "context": "Martín del Barco Centenera actively participated in several expeditions across the vast territories of South America between 1572 and 1582, contributing to its early mapping and understanding."}], "places": [{"name": "Logrosán, Spain", "role": "Birthplace"}, {"name": "Asunción, Paraguay", "role": "Archdeaconry location"}, {"name": "Lisbon, Portugal", "role": "Publication city"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Martín del Barco Centenera", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "martín-del-barco-centenera"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q2361449", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mart%C3%ADn_del_Barco_Centenera"], "tags": ["Spain", "Religious Figures", "Europe", "comparative-religion", "doctrine-development", "cultural-diffusion"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "Martín del Barco Centenera left a notable mark on Early Modern history, documented across 0 Wikipedia editions worldwide. Key lasting impacts include: Shaped religious practice and spiritual communities; Contributed to global exchange networks, modern state formation, and scientific revolution.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Martín del Barco Centenera's *La Argentina* is a vital primary source for the early Spanish colonial history of the Río de la Plata, offering unique ethnographic and geographical details that shaped regional historical understanding. His work provided a foundational narrative for what would become Argentina and Paraguay.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-11-21T08:15:38Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#44883698248", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Martín del Barco Centenera was a Spanish cleric, explorer and author.", "newValue": "Martín del Barco Centenera (1535, Logrosán – 1602) was a Spanish cleric, explorer, and author whose extensive travels and observations across South America culminated in the epic poem *La Argentina y Conquista del Río de la Plata*, published in 1602. His work stands as a foundational text for understanding the early Spanish colonial experience in the Río de la Plata region, providing a unique eyewitness account of the conquest, settlement, and ethnography of a vast, often turbulent, frontier during the late 16th century.

After arriving in the Viceroyalty of Peru in 1572 as part of the expedition led by Governor Juan Ortiz de Zárate, Barco Centenera served as archdeacon of the Cathedral of Asunción (present-day Paraguay) for a decade, from 1575 to 1582. During this period, he actively participated in various exploratory and military expeditions, including campaigns against indigenous groups and journeys across vast territories that would later form parts of Argentina, Paraguay, and Uruguay. His firsthand exposure to the diverse landscapes, indigenous cultures like the Guaraní and Charrua, and the challenges faced by Spanish settlers provided the rich material that would later animate his ambitious literary project.

